1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,270
Two blind men are sitting at the side of a road, and a crowd is coming.

2
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:21,863
Stay with them a moment, because they will hear the crowd long before anyone else
could see it. A crowd in a small town is not one sound. It is a hundred.

3
00:00:21,913 --> 00:00:30,907
Sandals on packed earth, first a few and then so many that the footsteps blur into
one long shuffle.

4
00:00:30,957 --> 00:00:43,114
Voices on top of voices, nobody shouting yet, everybody talking at once, the way
people talk when something has just happened and they can't believe what they saw.

5
00:00:43,164 --> 00:00:53,160
A child being carried and complaining about it. A dog. Snippets of excited
conversation. Fragments of a familiar name.

6
00:00:53,210 --> 00:01:05,406
Two blind men know what that sound means before anyone tells them. They have heard
the news for days, the synagogue ruler's daughter, dead and then not dead.

7
00:01:05,456 --> 00:01:18,050
Now the man who did it is walking past them, close enough to hear His sandals, and
He is not stopping. No, He is definitely not stopping. Why is He not stopping?

8
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:32,710
So they do the thing blind men almost never do in a crowd. They get up and walk
right into the middle of it.

9
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:48,212
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

10
00:01:48,262 --> 00:01:57,189
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

11
00:01:57,239 --> 00:02:01,046
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

12
00:02:01,096 --> 00:02:13,668
This episode gathers two short accounts from Matthew, a few chapters apart, about
blind people who did not wait by the road. In the first, two blind men follow Jesus.

13
00:02:13,718 --> 00:02:21,710
In the second, a man who can neither see nor speak is brought to Him, and an
argument breaks out over his head.

14
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:28,700
First, where we are. Matthew chapter nine.

15
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:33,275
Jesus has just done something that has set the whole region talking:

16
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:40,003
He has gone into the house of a synagogue leader named Jairus and raised his
daughter from the dead.

17
00:02:40,053 --> 00:02:43,739
Matthew says the news spread through all that land.

18
00:02:43,789 --> 00:02:56,770
As Jesus leaves that house, two men who cannot see have heard the news, and they do
not intend to let Him simply pass by. Matthew chapter 9, verses 27 to 31.

19
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:09,846
[Reader] As Jesus went on from there, two blind men followed Him, crying out, “Have
mercy on us, Son of David!” After Jesus had entered the house, the blind men came to
Him.

20
00:03:09,896 --> 00:03:15,613
[Reader] “Do you believe that I am able to do this?” He asked. “Yes, Lord,” they
answered.

21
00:03:15,663 --> 00:03:23,847
[Reader] Then He touched their eyes and said, “According to your faith will it be
done to you.” And their eyes were opened.

22
00:03:23,897 --> 00:03:32,350
[Reader] Jesus warned them sternly, “See that no one finds out about this!” But they
went out and spread the news about Him throughout the land.

23
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:46,993
Son of David. Matthew Henry notes that this was the common name for the Messiah, the
King promised to David's line. These two were not calling a healer.

24
00:03:47,043 --> 00:03:56,140
They were naming who they believed He was, in the street, in a town where that was a
dangerous thing to say.

25
00:03:56,190 --> 00:04:09,483
Henry pictures them following Him through the streets as beggars did, with their
incessant cries. He also notices the pronoun. They did not each say have mercy on
me.

26
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:23,110
They said have mercy on us. Two men in the same trouble, with one prayer between
them. Notice what Jesus does when they shout. He keeps walking.

27
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:32,839
He does not stop in the street, and He does not answer. The two men follow Him
anyway, all the way to the house and inside it.

28
00:04:32,889 --> 00:04:38,793
Only then does He turn and speak to them. Matthew Henry gives a reason.

29
00:04:38,843 --> 00:04:51,254
Jesus did not take notice of them at first, Henry says, because He meant to test
their faith, and He already knew it was strong. Then Henry adds something kind.

30
00:04:51,304 --> 00:04:59,362
Pushing into a house after a man who wanted to be alone looked rude. But Jesus did
not treat it as rude.

31
00:04:59,412 --> 00:05:14,282
In Henry's words, they were not more bold than welcome: as bold as they were, they
were just as welcome. So the shouting was not the test. The following was.

32
00:05:14,332 --> 00:05:17,790
Anyone can call out from the side of a road.

33
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:29,721
These two walked after Him down a street they could not see and through a door into
a crowded room, and the healing waited until they had done it.

34
00:05:29,771 --> 00:05:32,504
Where this happened is not certain.

35
00:05:32,554 --> 00:05:40,664
Edersheim places it on the way back into Capernaum, Jesus's home base on the north
shore of the Sea of Galilee,

36
00:05:40,714 --> 00:05:46,692
a town of stone houses with flat roofs and narrow lanes running down to the water.

37
00:05:46,742 --> 00:05:53,602
The house would have been one of those: a courtyard, a doorway, a dim room.

38
00:05:53,652 --> 00:06:08,774
Elsewhere Edersheim wonders whether the details point somewhere else, and does not
settle it. The place is uncertain. The path is not: road, street, door, house.

39
00:06:08,824 --> 00:06:19,899
Where this is. Capernaum sits at the north tip of the Sea of Galilee. Stand there
and face north, with north at twelve o'clock.

40
00:06:19,949 --> 00:06:27,807
Bethsaida, where we were last episode, is about two hours' walk at two o'clock,
round the top of the lake.

41
00:06:27,857 --> 00:06:39,380
Everything else we have visited is straight behind you at six o'clock, down the
Jordan valley: Jericho first, about three days' walk, and Jerusalem beyond it,

42
00:06:39,430 --> 00:06:47,290
about three and a half, which is what a Galilean pilgrim planned on for the road up
to a feast in Jerusalem.

43
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:54,890
A family with children took longer. On the map, this is the farthest north the
series has been.

44
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:10,160
Scripture tells us they followed and what they shouted. It does not tell us how two
blind men kept up with a moving crowd, or how they got through it at the door.

45
00:07:10,210 --> 00:07:20,670
We imagine two men who had worked the first part out long ago, and had to work the
second part out that day. This is a scene, not Scripture.

46
00:07:24,843 --> 00:07:33,270
[Man] Here they come. Hear it? That is not market noise. That is one crowd, all
going the same way.

47
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:43,430
[Friend] There is a center to it. A quiet place in the middle. Somebody they are all
following.

48
00:07:50,876 --> 00:08:00,130
[Man] It's him! Can't you hear it? Up. Hurry! Hand on my shoulder. Let's go! Stay
close. Son of David, have mercy on us!

49
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:03,410
[Friend] Yes! Son of David, have mercy on us!

50
00:08:05,960 --> 00:08:11,963
[Man] He is not stopping. The crowd is moving left, toward the lake road. Come on.
We do not need eyes for this part.

51
00:08:12,013 --> 00:08:15,910
[Man] The loudest thing in this town is telling us exactly where we want to go.

52
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:32,590
[Friend] Street now. Walls close on both sides, the sound bounces. The crowd is
bunching up ahead. They have stopped. A doorway, it must be. They are all trying to
get in.

53
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:51,007
[Man] And here is the hard part. Not the road. This. A crowd standing still is a
wall of backs and elbows, and if we push, we are the blind men knocking into
everyone.

54
00:08:51,057 --> 00:08:55,710
[Man] Every day of my life I would stop right here and wait.

55
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,150
[Friend] Not today. Have faith!

56
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:08,650
[Man] No. You are right. Not today. Keep your hand on me. Pardon us. Pardon us, we
are going in. Son of David!

57
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:17,710
[Voice] Mind the step. There is a step down here. Are you two following the teacher?
He has gone inside. Here. Give me your hand. Low lintel.

58
00:09:19,260 --> 00:09:26,730
[Friend] Dark in here. Cooler. Oil lamps. A lot of people breathing, and then they
go quiet.

59
00:09:22,144 --> 00:09:26,980
[Man] He has turned around. I can feel it. He is looking at us.

60
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:42,650
Do you believe I am able to do this? And they said, Yes, Lord.

61
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:57,512
And now, a word from Brian. Or rather, from the voice Brian picked for himself,
since he would not use his own. Around here we call it Mister Scaredy Cat.

62
00:09:57,562 --> 00:10:00,810
The voice is not his, but the words are.

63
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:12,271
[Brian] Two blind men following a crowd down a road and into a house. I want you to
picture how that actually works, because it is not what sighted people imagine.

64
00:10:12,321 --> 00:10:19,892
[Brian] You do not follow with your eyes, but you can hear which way the sounds are
going. You follow the sound of the crowd, and you follow each other.

65
00:10:19,942 --> 00:10:24,364
[Brian] One of them has a hand on the other's shoulder, or is holding an arm.

66
00:10:24,414 --> 00:10:33,486
[Brian] I have done exactly this countless times when it has just been me and a
blind brother or sister, and we work as a team and eventually get where we need to
be.

67
00:10:33,536 --> 00:10:38,922
[Brian] It is slower, but it works. Finding the crowd was never going to be their
problem.

68
00:10:38,972 --> 00:10:46,433
[Brian] A crowd that size is the loudest thing in a town, and a house with a crowd
inside it leaks noise out of every door and window.

69
00:10:46,483 --> 00:10:51,197
[Brian] Two blind men could have walked straight to it. The problem is the last ten
feet.

70
00:10:51,247 --> 00:10:56,277
[Brian] I have stood at the edge of a packed room more times than I can count, and
what I do is stop.

71
00:10:56,327 --> 00:11:06,256
[Brian] I stand still, because if I move I bump into people, and if I bump into
people I am the blind man knocking into everyone. Standing still is safe.

72
00:11:06,306 --> 00:11:13,314
[Brian] Those two men were desperate to get to Jesus, and they could not get to Him
by standing still. So they went into the crowd.

73
00:11:13,364 --> 00:11:23,675
[Brian] That is one of the bravest things in the whole story and it gets no verse at
all. Jesus did not stop on the road. He let them follow Him all the way into the
house.

74
00:11:23,725 --> 00:11:31,326
[Brian] I used to think that was a little hard. Now I think He was letting them do
the thing they could do, and maybe testing it. They could follow.

75
00:11:31,376 --> 00:11:38,582
[Brian] Every step of that road was them saying we believe you are worth following,
before He ever asked whether they believed.

76
00:11:38,632 --> 00:11:46,524
[Brian] By the time He asked the question, they had already answered it with their
feet. Then He asked. Do you believe I am able to do this?

77
00:11:46,574 --> 00:11:53,881
[Brian] He asked two blind men a question and waited for the answer. He did not ask
the crowd about them. He did not ask whoever was with them.

78
00:11:53,931 --> 00:12:00,150
[Brian] He did not assume that everyone knew what they wanted. He asked them. In my
experience that is often half the battle.

79
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:09,536
Why did Jesus tell them sternly to keep quiet?

80
00:12:09,586 --> 00:12:20,498
Henry lists four reasons the commentators had offered: humility; a judgment on
Capernaum, which had seen many miracles and not believed; caution,

81
00:12:20,548 --> 00:12:27,301
because the rulers were growing jealous; and so as not to stir the crowds before the
time.

82
00:12:27,351 --> 00:12:37,085
Edersheim's reading is that a confession as far-reaching as theirs, calling Him Son
of David and able to open blind eyes with a touch,

83
00:12:37,135 --> 00:12:39,714
was not yet to be shouted in public.

84
00:12:39,764 --> 00:12:47,919
The time for that would come, on a donkey, outside Jerusalem. But the men did not
keep quiet.

85
00:12:47,969 --> 00:12:58,046
They went out and spread the news through all that land, which, if you have ever
been given back something you thought was gone for good, you will understand.

86
00:12:58,096 --> 00:13:09,550
Matthew does not pause. In the very next verse, as those two are going out, another
man is being brought in. Matthew chapter 9, verses 32 to 34.

87
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:23,556
[Reader] As they were leaving, a demon-possessed man who was mute was brought to
Jesus. And when the demon had been driven out, the man began to speak.

88
00:13:23,606 --> 00:13:34,750
[Reader] The crowds were amazed and said, “Nothing like this has ever been seen in
Israel!” But the Pharisees said, “It is by the prince of demons that He drives out
demons.”

89
00:13:36,300 --> 00:13:47,390
Hold on to those two reactions, the crowd's and the Pharisees', because Matthew is
going to show them to us again, louder, three chapters later,

90
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,330
with a man who is both blind and mute.

91
00:13:50,380 --> 00:14:03,455
Matthew chapter twelve. We are still in Galilee, by Edersheim's reckoning, more than
a year before the end. By now the conflict with the Pharisees has hardened.

92
00:14:03,505 --> 00:14:11,523
Jesus has healed on the Sabbath and defended it, and Matthew says they have begun to
plot how to destroy Him.

93
00:14:11,573 --> 00:14:21,290
Into that, a man is brought who cannot see and cannot speak. Matthew chapter 12,
verses 22 to 28.

94
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:31,992
[Reader] Then a demon-possessed man who was blind and mute was brought to Jesus, and
He healed the man so that he could speak and see.

95
00:14:32,042 --> 00:14:37,575
[Reader] The crowds were astounded and asked, “Could this be the Son of David?”

96
00:14:37,625 --> 00:14:46,679
[Reader] But when the Pharisees heard this, they said, “Only by Beelzebul, the
prince of demons, does this man drive out demons.”

97
00:14:46,729 --> 00:14:58,720
[Reader] Knowing their thoughts, Jesus said to them, “Every kingdom divided against
itself will be laid waste, and every city or household divided against itself will
not stand.

98
00:14:58,770 --> 00:15:12,594
[Reader] If Satan drives out Satan, he is divided against himself. How then can his
kingdom stand? And if I drive out demons by Beelzebul, by whom do your sons drive
them out?

99
00:15:12,644 --> 00:15:21,510
[Reader] So then, they will be your judges. But if I drive out demons by the Spirit
of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.

100
00:15:23,274 --> 00:15:34,203
Count the verses. One for the healing. Six, and then the rest of the chapter, for
the argument about it. Matthew is not being careless with the man.

101
00:15:34,253 --> 00:15:38,106
He is showing us where the attention in that room went.

102
00:15:38,156 --> 00:15:49,375
A man who could neither see nor speak was made whole, and within a sentence the
people with power were talking about Jesus, and about themselves,

103
00:15:49,425 --> 00:15:51,395
and the man was forgotten.

104
00:15:51,445 --> 00:15:59,933
Edersheim keeps this man apart from the mute man of chapter nine. That first healing
came early, when the crowds were still amazed.

105
00:15:59,983 --> 00:16:11,775
This one, which Luke also tells, came much later, after the Pharisees had settled on
their explanation: that Jesus did His works by the prince of demons.

106
00:16:11,825 --> 00:16:21,442
So by the time this man is carried in, the argument is already waiting for him. That
is why he disappears from the story the moment he can speak.

107
00:16:21,492 --> 00:16:30,811
The room had stopped looking at him before he opened his mouth. Stay with the man
himself for a moment, because the text does not.

108
00:16:30,861 --> 00:16:34,503
Blind, he cannot find his own way to Jesus.

109
00:16:34,553 --> 00:16:44,455
Mute, he cannot ask for anything when he gets there, or tell anyone what he wants,
or answer the question Jesus asked the two men in the house.

110
00:16:44,505 --> 00:16:52,410
Everything that the two blind men did for themselves, shouting, following,
answering, this man cannot do.

111
00:16:52,460 --> 00:17:00,974
Someone has to carry him, and someone has to speak for him, and he has no way to say
whether they have spoken rightly.

112
00:17:01,024 --> 00:17:08,305
In a world with no sign language that anyone recorded, and no way to write for a man
who could not see,

113
00:17:08,355 --> 00:17:13,998
a hand on his arm may have been the whole of his conversation with other people.

114
00:17:14,048 --> 00:17:21,539
Matthew gives his healing one verse. It may be the loneliest verse in the Gospels.

115
00:17:21,589 --> 00:17:32,121
Now a word about the phrase demon-possessed, because a blind listener may hear it
with a wince, as if Scripture were saying that blindness is the devil's work.

116
00:17:32,171 --> 00:17:42,715
It is not saying that, and it helps to know why. First, the words. Edersheim points
out that the Gospels never actually say possessed.

117
00:17:42,765 --> 00:17:51,353
Their word is demonised, and the language of possession comes from Josephus, the
historian, not from Matthew or Luke.

118
00:17:51,403 --> 00:18:05,135
Second, the Gospels keep two things apart that we tend to run together. Being blind,
or deaf, or mute, or paralysed was not, by itself, called being demonised.

119
00:18:05,185 --> 00:18:15,770
The lists of people Jesus healed name the sick and the demonised as separate groups,
again and again. Blindness on its own was an illness.

120
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:19,490
This one man is described as both, and that is unusual.

121
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:37,675
Third, the times. The 1915 Bible encyclopedia notes that across the ancient Near
East, eye disease was widely thought to be sent by God.

122
00:18:37,725 --> 00:18:50,441
People then would have assumed a cause for this man's condition. Matthew does not.
He gives no cause at all for the blindness, no sin, no judgment, nothing.

123
00:18:50,491 --> 00:18:56,166
He gives the result: the man could speak and see.

124
00:18:56,216 --> 00:19:06,190
The only time the Gospels record anyone asking Jesus whose sin caused a blindness,
in John chapter nine, He answered, neither.

125
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:11,524
[Brian] The second man could not see and could not speak.

126
00:19:11,574 --> 00:19:19,075
[Brian] Somebody had to bring him, and somebody had to speak for him, and then once
he could speak, the argument started over his head and he never got a line.

127
00:19:19,125 --> 00:19:28,048
[Brian] I notice that every time I read it. The man gets his sight and his voice,
and the Pharisees immediately make it about themselves. I have been in that room.

128
00:19:28,098 --> 00:19:31,870
[Brian] Not healed, but talked over. It is a particularly frustrating feeling.

129
00:19:38,041 --> 00:19:42,177
Put the two accounts side by side and notice the pattern.

130
00:19:42,227 --> 00:19:52,824
In both, the crowd asks the right question, could this be the Son of David, the very
title the two blind men shouted in the street.

131
00:19:52,874 --> 00:20:06,831
In both, the Pharisees answer with the same charge. And in both, the blind people
themselves are the ones who got it right first. The two men said it on the road.

132
00:20:06,881 --> 00:20:18,270
The crowd said it after the healing. The Pharisees never said it at all. By the end
of chapter twelve, Jesus will tell them that their problem is not their eyes.

133
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:25,538
[Brian] I think about my friends who have lost both sight and hearing, the deaf-
blind.

134
00:20:25,588 --> 00:20:33,585
[Brian] This man in Matthew was blind and mute, which is a different pairing, but
the wall is the same: you cannot take the world in, or you cannot give anything
back,

135
00:20:33,635 --> 00:20:35,761
[Brian] and either way you are cut off from people.

136
00:20:35,811 --> 00:20:44,645
[Brian] In that world, as far as I know, there was no manual alphabet and no sign
language that anyone wrote down. Helen Keller had a teacher and a system.

137
00:20:44,695 --> 00:20:53,392
[Brian] A man like this had nothing, and the people who loved him had nothing to
reach him with except a hand on his arm.

138
00:20:53,442 --> 00:21:03,375
[Brian] I remind myself of that whenever I feel sorry for myself. My hearing is
excellent, and my brain has learned to use sound in ways most sighted people never
will.

139
00:21:03,425 --> 00:21:11,150
[Brian] Blindness is a spectrum, and so is this. There were people in that crowd far
worse off than me, and somebody carried one of them to Jesus.

140
00:21:15,537 --> 00:21:23,506
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at blindness
in the Bible dot com.

141
00:21:23,556 --> 00:21:34,999
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

142
00:21:35,049 --> 00:21:42,230
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

